    const Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content: 'An 18-year-old female with L3 myelomeningocele presents to the PM&R transition clinic for establishment of adult care. She was followed in the pediatric spina bifida clinic since birth and has "aged out" of the program. Her surgical history includes myelomeningocele repair at 1 day of life, VP shunt placement at 2 weeks (revised twice — most recently at age 8 for proximal obstruction), bilateral hip osteotomies at age 6, and a Mitrofanoff appendicovesicostomy at age 10 for bladder management. She ambulates with bilateral KAFOs and Lofstrand crutches for household distances and uses a lightweight manual wheelchair for community mobility. She performs clean intermittent catheterization (CIC) via the Mitrofanoff channel every 4 hours and uses a timed bowel program with digital stimulation and bisacodyl suppository every other day. She is a senior in high school, has been accepted to a state university 3 hours from home, and plans to study graphic design. She has a 3.5 GPA, has passed her driver\'s test using hand controls, and has an active social media presence where she advocates for disability awareness. Her parents, particularly her father, are deeply involved in her care and have accompanied her to every medical appointment. During today\'s visit, she arrives independently in her wheelchair. Her parents are in the waiting room and ask to come back with her. She tells the front desk staff: "I\'d like to go back alone first." When you meet with her privately, she says: "I need to figure out how to do this on my own. My parents still treat me like I\'m twelve, and they don\'t want me to go away for college. My dad says it\'s too dangerous. I love them, but I need my own life."',
