    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: '**Transition-Specific Assessment (Comprehensive)**:\n\n**Medical Complexity in Adult Spina Bifida**:\n- **Tethered cord syndrome**: Occurs in up to 30% of repaired myelomeningocele patients, can present at any age with new neurologic symptoms. Monitor for new back pain, change in gait, worsening lower extremity strength, change in bladder function, progressive scoliosis, or new foot deformity.\n- **VP shunt malfunction**: Lifelong risk. Symptoms in adults may be subtle: headaches, cognitive changes, vision changes, personality changes, nausea. Adult emergency departments frequently miss shunt malfunction because they are less familiar with it than pediatric EDs.\n- **Latex allergy**: Present in up to 50-70% of spina bifida patients due to early and repeated surgical exposure. Must be documented prominently in all medical records. Life-threatening anaphylaxis risk. All medical procedures must be performed in latex-free environment.\n- **Chiari II malformation**: Present in virtually all myelomeningocele patients. May be asymptomatic or cause headaches, dysphagia, sleep apnea, upper extremity weakness. MRI if new symptoms.\n\n**Bladder Management Review**:\n- CIC via Mitrofanoff every 4 hours — assess technique, compliance, and independence\n- Renal ultrasound annually — monitor for hydronephrosis, renal calculi, renal scarring\n- Urodynamics every 1-2 years — assess bladder compliance, detrusor pressures (>40 cmH2O risks upper tract damage), capacity, and continence\n- Anticholinergic medication review: Is she on oxybutynin or solifenacin? Side effects (dry mouth, constipation, cognitive effects)\n- Continence status: Social continence is critical for college life. Any incontinence episodes?\n- UTI frequency: Recurrent UTIs are common. Distinguish colonization (asymptomatic bacteriuria, common and does NOT require treatment) from true infection (new symptoms: fever, increased spasticity, foul-smelling urine, abdominal pain, leukocytosis)\n\n**Bowel Management Review**:\n- Current program effectiveness: continent between programs? Time to complete? Any skin breakdown from incontinence?\n- Transition to independent bowel program management: Can she perform digital stimulation and suppository insertion independently?\n- Dietary fiber and fluid intake optimization\n- Consider transanal irrigation (Peristeen system) if current program is inefficient for college schedule\n\n**Skin and Pressure Injury Risk**:\n- L3 myelomeningocele: Sensation absent below L3 (absent over lateral leg, foot, perianal region)\n- Insensate skin over buttocks, perineum, and feet — lifetime risk of pressure injuries\n- Wheelchair cushion assessment: pressure mapping to optimize cushion selection\n- Skin check routine: Does she perform daily skin checks of all insensate areas? Use a mirror for areas she cannot see directly?\n- Foot care: Insensate feet are at risk in KAFOs — inspect feet after each use\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- L3 level motor function: Hip flexion and adduction preserved (L1-L3). Knee extension partially preserved (L3-L4). Absent: ankle dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, hip abduction, hip extension, knee flexion\n- KAFO condition: Inspect for wear, fit, and alignment\n- Upper extremity overuse injuries: Shoulder impingement, rotator cuff pathology, carpal tunnel syndrome — common in long-term crutch and wheelchair users\n- Obesity: Weight management is critical — limited lower extremity exercise capacity makes weight gain common\n\n**Psychosocial and Sexual Health**:\n- Depression and anxiety screening (PHQ-9, GAD-7)\n- Sexual health counseling: Sensation, fertility, safe sex practices, gynecologic care. Women with spina bifida have normal fertility. Pregnancy is possible but high-risk (folic acid supplementation critical, obstetric consultation needed).\n- Relationship history and body image assessment\n- Substance use screening (CRAFFT for adolescents)',
