      content: '**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, Weight 72 kg, Height 160 cm (measured supine), BMI 28.1 kg/m2 (overweight). Well-healed lumbar scar from myelomeningocele repair.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing young woman, articulate, confident demeanor. Arrives independently in lightweight manual wheelchair.\n\n**Neurologic**:\n- **Motor**: Hip flexors 4/5 bilaterally, hip adductors 4/5, knee extensors 3+/5 bilaterally, knee flexors 1/5 bilaterally, tibialis anterior 0/5, gastrocnemius/soleus 0/5, EHL 0/5, hip abductors 2/5, hip extensors 1/5\n- **Sensory**: Intact sensation to L3 dermatome (anterior thigh). Absent sensation below L3: lateral thigh (L3-4 border), below knee, feet, perianal region, perineum\n- **Reflexes**: Patellar trace bilaterally. Achilles absent. Bulbocavernosus reflex absent.\n- **Tone**: Flaccid bilateral lower extremities below L3\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- Bilateral KAFOs in good condition, mild wear on medial uprights\n- Mild bilateral knee flexion contractures (lacks 10 degrees of full extension)\n- Bilateral ankle plantigrade in AFO, no significant equinus\n- Mild thoracolumbar levoscoliosis (Cobb angle ~15 degrees, stable per prior imaging)\n- Bilateral shoulder examination: Mild bilateral supraspinatus tenderness, positive Neer impingement sign on left, full ROM but painful arc on left. Likely early rotator cuff tendinopathy from wheelchair propulsion.\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Well-healed scars over bilateral hips (osteotomy), lumbar spine, abdomen (Mitrofanoff). Mitrofanoff stoma in right lower quadrant, clean and patent, no granulation tissue.\n\n**Abdominal**: Soft, non-distended. VP shunt tubing palpable in right abdomen. No tenderness.\n\n**Wheelchair Assessment**: Lightweight rigid-frame manual wheelchair, 16-inch seat width, gel cushion (no pressure mapping data available), ROHO back support. Propulsion mechanics: mild forward trunk lean, asymmetric push pattern (right dominant). Wheelchair is 3 years old and showing frame wear.\n\n**Functional Mobility**:\n- Wheelchair-to-mat transfer: Independent, sliding board not used\n- Floor-to-wheelchair: Independent using step-up technique against furniture\n- Ambulation with bilateral KAFOs and Lofstrand crutches: Swing-through gait pattern, energy-intensive. Walks approximately 100 feet before requiring rest. Uses ambulation primarily at home.\n- Car transfer: Independent. Drives with hand controls independently.',
