    const Section(
      title: '+ Outline the essential components of an adult spina bifida transition care plan, including the monitoring schedule and common adult complications.',
      content: '**Adult Spina Bifida — Transition Care Plan**:\n\n**Medical Passport Document**:\nCreate a comprehensive medical summary that the patient carries and provides to all new providers. This is critical because most adult providers have limited experience with spina bifida. Include:\n- Complete surgical history with dates\n- VP shunt type, location, and revision history\n- Current level of lesion and functional status\n- Latex allergy status (PROMINENTLY marked)\n- Bladder and bowel program details\n- Baseline neurologic examination\n- Current medications\n- Allergies\n- Emergency contacts and key providers\n- Baseline imaging (most recent brain MRI, spinal MRI, renal ultrasound)\n\n**Annual Monitoring Schedule**:\n\n| System | Assessment | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Renal | Ultrasound, BUN/Creatinine, GFR | Annually |\n| Bladder | Urodynamics | Every 1-2 years |\n| Neurologic | Comprehensive exam, compare to baseline | Annually |\n| Shunt | Baseline brain imaging (CT or MRI), then PRN if symptoms | Baseline + PRN |\n| Skin | Full body skin check, pressure mapping | Annually |\n| Musculoskeletal | Joint ROM, KAFO/wheelchair assessment, scoliosis | Annually |\n| Metabolic | BMI, lipids, glucose, vitamin D | Annually |\n| Gynecologic | Pap smear, pelvic exam (adapted positioning) | Per guidelines |\n| Psychological | Depression/anxiety screening | Annually |\n| Upper extremity | Shoulder and wrist assessment for overuse | Annually |\n\n**Common Adult Complications to Monitor**:\n\n1. **Tethered cord**: New symptoms warranting emergent MRI: worsening back pain, new lower extremity weakness, change in bladder or bowel function, progressive scoliosis, new foot deformity. Surgical untethering may be needed. Maintain a documented baseline neurologic exam for comparison.\n\n2. **VP shunt malfunction**: Annual baseline brain imaging (CT or fast MRI) to document stable ventricle size. If symptoms develop (headache, cognitive change, nausea, vision changes), compare to baseline. Adult EDs must be educated about this patient\'s shunt. Provide a "shunt card" with shunt details and neurosurgery contact information.\n\n3. **Renal deterioration**: Leading historical cause of death in spina bifida before modern urologic management. Monitor upper tracts vigilantly. Detrusor pressures >40 cmH2O on urodynamics require intervention (medication adjustment, botulinum toxin to detrusor, bladder augmentation).\n\n4. **Obesity**: Affects up to 50% of adults with spina bifida. Limited exercise capacity, lower basal metabolic rate. Dietitian referral. Explore accessible exercise options: hand cycling, wheelchair sports, swimming.\n\n5. **Pressure injuries**: Insensate skin + sitting + limited mobility = high risk. Pressure mapping every 1-2 years. Wheelchair cushion replacement every 2-3 years or sooner if worn. Weight shifts every 15-30 minutes.\n\n6. **Upper extremity overuse**: Rotator cuff tendinopathy, CTS, elbow pathology from wheelchair propulsion and crutch use. May eventually limit manual wheelchair use — consider power assist wheels or power wheelchair if upper extremity pain limits function.\n\n7. **Depression and social isolation**: Rates of depression are 2-3x higher than general population. Transition from structured pediatric care to fragmented adult care is a high-risk period. Screen actively.\n\n**Sexual and Reproductive Health**:\n- Women with L3 myelomeningocele typically have intact fertility\n- Pregnancy is possible but high-risk: folic acid 4 mg/day (high-dose for neural tube defect history), shunt monitoring during pregnancy, cesarean delivery often needed, latex precautions during delivery\n- Sensation: L3 level means absent genital sensation. Sexual counseling should address alternative erogenous zones, communication with partners, and the emotional aspects of intimacy.\n- Gynecologic care: May need adapted positioning for pelvic exams (side-lying). Pap smears per standard guidelines.\n- Contraception counseling: Avoid estrogen-containing methods if immobile (DVT risk). Progesterone-only, IUD, or barrier methods preferred.',
