    const Section(
      title: '+ Discuss SSI/SSDI eligibility, vocational rehabilitation services, and college disability services for a young adult with spina bifida.',
      content: '**SSI/SSDI and Financial Support**:\n\n**Supplemental Security Income (SSI)**:\n- Needs-based program for individuals with limited income and resources who are disabled\n- Children with spina bifida often receive SSI, but eligibility is re-evaluated at age 18 using adult criteria\n- **Age-18 redetermination**: At 18, SSA applies adult disability standards (ability to engage in substantial gainful activity). Many pediatric SSI recipients lose benefits at this redetermination if their functional status allows employment.\n- This patient is high-functioning (drives, attends college, independent in ADLs). She may not meet adult SSI disability criteria.\n- **Important**: SSI income limits may affect eligibility. If she works part-time, earnings above the substantial gainful activity threshold (\$1,620/month in 2025) could disqualify her.\n\n**Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)**:\n- Requires sufficient work history (earnings credits). An 18-year-old typically has insufficient work history.\n- **SSDI as an adult child**: If a parent receives SSDI or has died, a disabled adult child (disability onset before age 22) may qualify for benefits on the parent\'s record. This patient\'s onset is clearly before age 22.\n- Worth exploring with a Social Security benefits counselor\n\n**Plan to Achieve Self-Support (PASS)**:\n- An SSA work incentive that allows SSI recipients to set aside income and resources for a work goal (such as education)\n- Could allow her to attend college and work part-time without losing all SSI benefits\n- Requires a written PASS plan approved by SSA\n\n**Vocational Rehabilitation (VR) Services**:\n- State-federal program that assists individuals with disabilities in achieving employment\n- **Services available**: Career counseling, job training, college tuition assistance, adaptive equipment, job placement, transportation, on-the-job support\n- **Eligibility**: Must have a disability that results in an impediment to employment and must be able to benefit from VR services in terms of employment outcome\n- This patient should be referred to VR as early as possible — ideally age 16 (transition planning under IDEA), but 18 is still appropriate\n- VR can fund: tuition not covered by financial aid, adaptive computer equipment, ergonomic workstation, transportation to campus, other disability-related educational expenses\n- **Individual Plan for Employment (IPE)**: Developed collaboratively between the VR counselor and the client. Sets the employment goal (graphic designer) and the services needed to get there.\n\n**College Disability Services**:\n- **Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act** and **Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)**: Federal laws requiring colleges to provide reasonable accommodations to students with documented disabilities\n- The student must self-identify and provide documentation (medical records, functional limitation statements)\n- The college does NOT have to provide the same level of support as K-12 (no IEP equivalent). The student is responsible for self-advocacy.\n- Common accommodations: accessible housing, classroom accessibility, exam accommodations, note-taking assistance, priority registration\n\n**Financial Aid**:\n- FAFSA: File for federal financial aid. Disability does not directly increase aid, but medical expenses may affect the Expected Family Contribution.\n- Scholarships: Numerous disability-specific scholarships exist (National Federation of the Blind, Spina Bifida Association scholarships, Organization for Autism Research, etc.)\n- Spina Bifida Association of America: Scholarship program specifically for students with spina bifida\n\n**Health Insurance**:\n- If currently on parent\'s insurance: ACA allows dependent coverage until age 26\n- If on Medicaid: Check state rules for continued eligibility during college. Some states have Medicaid buy-in programs for working individuals with disabilities.\n- College student health insurance: Evaluate whether it covers specialty care, DME, and prescriptions needed. May need to maintain parent\'s insurance as primary.',
