    const Section(
      title: '+ Navigate the tension between an overprotective parent and an adult patient seeking independence, while preserving the family relationship.',
      content: '**Step 1 — Acknowledge the Father\'s Love and Sacrifice**:\n"[Father\'s name], before I say anything else, I want to acknowledge what you have done for your daughter. Eighteen years of medical appointments, surgeries, therapy, advocating with schools and insurance companies, learning how to manage a catheter program, sitting in hospital waiting rooms — you have been an extraordinary father. The fact that she is sitting here today, accepted to college with a 3.5 GPA, driving a car, and advocating for herself, is a direct result of the foundation you built. You should be incredibly proud."\n\n**Step 2 — Validate the Fear**:\n"I also want to say that your concerns are medically legitimate. Shunt malfunctions happen. UTIs happen. Pressure injuries happen. These are real risks, and you are not being irrational. You know better than most parents what is at stake because you have lived it."\n\n**Step 3 — Reframe the Risk Equation**:\n"But I want to offer you a different way to think about this. The greatest risk to your daughter\'s health and well-being over the next 50 years is not a shunt malfunction or a UTI. Those are manageable medical events. The greatest risk is dependency — not developing the skills and confidence to manage her own health, navigate her own emergencies, and build her own life. Every young adult with spina bifida who thrives in adulthood went through a scary transition period where their parents had to step back and let them practice being independent. And the parents who did that — even though it was terrifying — gave their children the greatest gift after their initial care."\n\n**Step 4 — Give the Patient Her Voice**:\n"[Patient\'s name], I think your father needs to hear from you. Can you tell him what you told me — what you want and what you are prepared to do?"\n[Allow her to speak. She has demonstrated the maturity and self-advocacy to do this. Your role is to create the space.]\n\n**Step 5 — Present the Safety Net**:\n"Let me tell you what we are going to do to make this as safe as possible. We are going to establish her with a neurosurgeon, a urologist, and a PM&R physician near campus before she leaves. She will have a medical alert identification for her latex allergy and shunt. She will have a medical passport that any emergency room can reference. Her roommate will be educated about emergencies. She will have a supply stockpile. We will do a self-management skills assessment and fill any gaps before she goes. She will not be starting from zero — she will be starting with 18 years of training that you gave her."\n\n**Step 6 — Propose a Graduated Approach**:\n"Would it help if we thought about this in stages? What if she spends the summer practicing fully independent medical self-management at home — you step back completely from her catheter schedule, her bowel program, her skin checks, her medication management? You are there as a safety net but not doing it for her. If she manages the summer independently, that builds confidence for both of you. And when she goes to college, we set up weekly check-in calls between her and our clinic for the first semester, just to make sure everything is on track."\n\n**Step 7 — Address the Unspoken Loss**:\n"I also want to gently say this: letting go of a caregiving role you have held for 18 years is a loss. It changes your identity as a parent. That is real, and it is okay to grieve that even as you celebrate her independence. Some parents find it helpful to talk to a counselor during this transition — not because something is wrong, but because it is a big change for the whole family."\n\n**Step 8 — Unite the Family**:\n"You are both on the same team. You both want her to be safe, healthy, and happy. The question is not whether she goes to college — she has earned that. The question is how we prepare her and support her so that she succeeds. Let us build that plan together."\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- The patient is a legal adult at 18 — her decisions are hers. But destroying the family relationship in the process of asserting autonomy serves no one.\n- Honor the parent\'s history while redirecting their energy from protection to preparation\n- Frame independence as the ultimate outcome of good parenting, not a rejection of it\n- Provide concrete safety measures that address specific fears\n- Offer a graduated approach that respects both the patient\'s autonomy and the parent\'s need for reassurance\n- Acknowledge the parent\'s grief in the role transition',
